1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,860
At noon on 14 October 1944, a dark car
stops outside a house in Herrlingen

2
00:00:05,860 --> 00:00:07,280
near Ulm.

3
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:09,260
Two generals get out.

4
00:00:09,260 --> 00:00:10,510
They do not stay long.

5
00:00:10,510 --> 00:00:14,700
Shortly afterwards, the master of the house
leaves with them and gets into the car.

6
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:16,790
A little later, he is dead.

7
00:00:16,790 --> 00:00:20,870
Officially, he is said to have
died of the effects of a wound.

8
00:00:20,870 --> 00:00:23,720
There is a state funeral
with full honours.

9
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:25,330
But that was a lie.

10
00:00:25,330 --> 00:00:28,530
The man was Field Marshal Erwin Rommel,

11
00:00:28,530 --> 00:00:32,430
and hardly any German soldier has
been written about, admired and

12
00:00:32,430 --> 00:00:34,110
argued over as much.

13
00:00:34,110 --> 00:00:38,060
Who was he really,
and how did he become a legend?

14
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:42,740
To understand Rommel,
we have to go back to October 1917.

15
00:00:42,740 --> 00:00:47,340
A young first lieutenant from Württemberg
leads a small detachment through the mountains

16
00:00:47,340 --> 00:00:48,880
on the Italian front.

17
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:52,770
His soldiers have been on the move
for days with almost no sleep.

18
00:00:52,770 --> 00:00:57,270
They bypass positions instead of
attacking them head-on, and appear

19
00:00:57,270 --> 00:00:59,020
where nobody expects them.

20
00:00:59,020 --> 00:01:02,360
In the end, they stand on
the summit of Monte Matajur.

21
00:01:02,360 --> 00:01:06,360
By his own account, Rommel's
unit took around 9,000 Italian

22
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:08,840
soldiers prisoner in those days.

23
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:13,570
He received the Pour le Mérite,
Prussia's highest award for bravery.

24
00:01:13,570 --> 00:01:19,690
He was born in 1891 in Heidenheim
an der Brenz, the son of a teacher.

25
00:01:19,690 --> 00:01:22,680
Nothing pointed to a military family.

26
00:01:22,680 --> 00:01:28,310
But it was these days in the mountains that
shaped him: be fast, lead from the front,

27
00:01:28,310 --> 00:01:29,920
surprise the enemy.

28
00:01:29,920 --> 00:01:32,820
Twenty years later he
wrote a book about it.

29
00:01:32,820 --> 00:01:35,190
It was called Infantry Attacks,

30
00:01:35,190 --> 00:01:39,400
became a bestseller and made him known
all the way to the top leadership.

31
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:41,470
The book opened doors for him.

32
00:01:41,470 --> 00:01:44,370
In 1939, during the attack on Poland,

33
00:01:44,370 --> 00:01:49,240
Rommel was responsible for the security
of the supreme headquarters, and so was

34
00:01:49,240 --> 00:01:51,890
close to the head of state every day.

35
00:01:51,890 --> 00:01:56,060
Afterwards he wanted a command
of his own, and he got one that

36
00:01:56,060 --> 00:01:59,670
hardly anyone thought suited him:
a panzer division.

37
00:01:59,670 --> 00:02:03,440
Rommel was an infantryman and
had never commanded tanks.

38
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:07,080
In May 1940, Germany attacks France.

39
00:02:07,080 --> 00:02:12,630
Rommel's 7th Panzer Division crosses the
Meuse at Dinant and pushes west so fast

40
00:02:12,630 --> 00:02:17,030
that at times even its own
command does not know where it is.

41
00:02:17,030 --> 00:02:20,040
Soon it is called the Ghost Division.

42
00:02:20,040 --> 00:02:22,100
Once again Rommel leads from

43
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:25,650
the front, often from a
tank turret or a radio car.

44
00:02:25,650 --> 00:02:30,070
His division reaches the Channel
coast and later takes Cherbourg.

45
00:02:30,070 --> 00:02:33,460
But the rapid advance also has a downside.

46
00:02:33,460 --> 00:02:38,710
The 7th Panzer Division suffers heavy
losses in this campaign, and Rommel tends to

47
00:02:38,710 --> 00:02:41,860
play down the achievements
of neighbouring units.

48
00:02:41,860 --> 00:02:47,030
Already here something begins that will follow
him through the whole war: Rommel understands

49
00:02:47,030 --> 00:02:48,970
how important images are.

50
00:02:48,970 --> 00:02:52,170
Cameras and war
correspondents accompany him,

51
00:02:52,170 --> 00:02:54,640
and at home his name becomes famous.

52
00:02:54,640 --> 00:02:58,660
In February 1941, Rommel lands in Libya.

53
00:02:58,660 --> 00:03:01,970
The Italian troops are close to collapse,

54
00:03:01,970 --> 00:03:06,570
and Berlin sends a small German
corps, the German Afrika Korps.

55
00:03:06,570 --> 00:03:10,520
The orders are clear: defend and wait.

56
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:12,370
Rommel attacks anyway.

57
00:03:12,370 --> 00:03:16,950
Within a few weeks he drives the
British back to the Egyptian border.

58
00:03:16,950 --> 00:03:19,730
Only one town holds out: Tobruk.

59
00:03:19,730 --> 00:03:22,910
For months he besieges the port in vain.

60
00:03:22,910 --> 00:03:26,350
In the desert, one thing
counts above all: supplies.

61
00:03:26,350 --> 00:03:28,130
Every litre of fuel and

62
00:03:28,130 --> 00:03:33,390
every shell has to be brought across the
Mediterranean and then hundreds of kilometres along

63
00:03:33,390 --> 00:03:35,170
a single coastal road.

64
00:03:35,170 --> 00:03:38,660
Rommel often plans further
than his supplies can reach.

65
00:03:38,660 --> 00:03:42,190
Even so, his reputation
grows, on both sides.

66
00:03:42,190 --> 00:03:45,780
In the British press he
gets a nickname that will

67
00:03:45,780 --> 00:03:48,310
never leave him: the Desert Fox.

68
00:03:48,310 --> 00:03:51,250
British soldiers talk about
him so often that their

69
00:03:51,250 --> 00:03:54,580
commander-in-chief, Claude
Auchinleck, sends a letter

70
00:03:54,580 --> 00:03:56,110
to his commanders.

71
00:03:56,110 --> 00:03:57,410
In it he warns that

72
00:03:57,410 --> 00:04:02,260
Rommel is becoming a kind of
magician or bogeyman to his own men.

73
00:04:02,260 --> 00:04:06,640
They should no longer speak of Rommel,
but simply of the Germans.

74
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:10,540
In June 1942, Tobruk finally falls,

75
00:04:10,540 --> 00:04:14,130
and more than 30,000
soldiers are taken prisoner.

76
00:04:14,130 --> 00:04:17,030
Rommel is promoted to field marshal.

77
00:04:17,030 --> 00:04:20,480
At 50, he is the youngest
in the German army.

78
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:22,710
But Rommel has gone too far.

79
00:04:22,710 --> 00:04:28,530
At El Alamein, about 100 kilometres from
Alexandria, the advance grinds to a halt.

80
00:04:28,530 --> 00:04:32,140
On the other side, Bernard
Montgomery takes command,

81
00:04:32,140 --> 00:04:36,650
and the British receive more and
more tanks, aircraft and fuel.

82
00:04:36,650 --> 00:04:43,130
When the great battle begins in October
1942, Rommel is not even in Africa.

83
00:04:43,130 --> 00:04:46,910
He is ill, taking a cure in
Austria, and rushes back.

84
00:04:46,910 --> 00:04:50,950
After twelve days it is clear
that the front cannot be held.

85
00:04:50,950 --> 00:04:56,370
Then an order arrives from headquarters:
not one step back, victory or death.

86
00:04:56,370 --> 00:05:00,420
Rommel hesitates for a day,
then retreats anyway.

87
00:05:00,420 --> 00:05:04,400
Half a year later, in May 1943, the German

88
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:08,330
and Italian troops in Tunisia surrender.

89
00:05:08,330 --> 00:05:12,200
Around 250,000 soldiers
are taken prisoner.

90
00:05:12,200 --> 00:05:15,570
By then, Rommel himself
is no longer in Africa.

91
00:05:15,570 --> 00:05:19,680
At the end of 1943,
Rommel is given a new task.

92
00:05:19,680 --> 00:05:24,090
He is to defend the French coast
against the expected Allied landing.

93
00:05:24,090 --> 00:05:28,550
He has millions of mines laid and
obstacles set up on the beaches,

94
00:05:28,550 --> 00:05:32,990
slanted stakes and steel crosses
meant to rip open landing craft.

95
00:05:32,990 --> 00:05:36,640
In the meadows behind them stand
wooden poles against gliders;

96
00:05:36,640 --> 00:05:39,330
the soldiers call them Rommel's asparagus.

97
00:05:39,330 --> 00:05:40,760
Rommel is convinced the

98
00:05:40,760 --> 00:05:45,000
battle will be decided on the
beach in the first 24 hours.

99
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,780
That is why he wants the
tanks close to the coast.

100
00:05:47,780 --> 00:05:50,990
Other generals want to hold
them back further inland.

101
00:05:50,990 --> 00:05:54,510
In the end there is a compromise
that follows neither plan properly.

102
00:05:54,510 --> 00:06:00,500
And on the morning of 6 June 1944,
Rommel is not in Normandy.

103
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:04,580
He is at home in Herrlingen,
because it is his wife Lucie's birthday.

104
00:06:04,580 --> 00:06:09,130
A few weeks after the landing,
Rommel considers the war lost and says so

105
00:06:09,130 --> 00:06:10,860
openly to his superiors.

106
00:06:10,860 --> 00:06:16,450
On 17 July 1944, an Allied
fighter plane attacks his car

107
00:06:16,450 --> 00:06:17,530
in Normandy.

108
00:06:17,530 --> 00:06:19,780
Rommel is badly injured in the head.

109
00:06:19,780 --> 00:06:23,690
Three days later, the assassination
attempt of 20 July fails,

110
00:06:23,690 --> 00:06:26,020
and a great wave of arrests begins.

111
00:06:26,020 --> 00:06:28,320
Rommel's name comes up too.

112
00:06:28,320 --> 00:06:31,510
How much he really knew
is disputed to this day.

113
00:06:31,510 --> 00:06:36,170
What is certain is that men around him,
such as his chief of staff Hans Speidel,

114
00:06:36,170 --> 00:06:38,150
were in contact with the conspirators.

115
00:06:38,150 --> 00:06:42,060
Whether Rommel would have supported
an assassination cannot be proven.

116
00:06:42,060 --> 00:06:45,070
For the leadership in Berlin,
suspicion was enough.

117
00:06:45,070 --> 00:06:50,140
Which brings us back to Herrlingen,
on 14 October 1944.

118
00:06:50,140 --> 00:06:55,550
The two generals, Wilhelm Burgdorf and
Ernst Maisel, bring Rommel a choice.

119
00:06:55,550 --> 00:06:58,610
Either a trial before the People's Court,

120
00:06:58,610 --> 00:07:02,450
with consequences for his
family and his staff, or poison.

121
00:07:02,450 --> 00:07:06,100
If he chooses poison,
his wife and son will be spared,

122
00:07:06,100 --> 00:07:08,240
and he will receive a state funeral.

123
00:07:08,240 --> 00:07:12,810
Rommel says goodbye to Lucie and
his 15-year-old son Manfred and gets

124
00:07:12,810 --> 00:07:14,110
into the car.

125
00:07:14,110 --> 00:07:17,630
Just outside the village,
he takes a cyanide capsule.

126
00:07:17,630 --> 00:07:23,030
Four days later, Field Marshal Gerd von
Rundstedt gives the funeral speech in Ulm.

127
00:07:23,030 --> 00:07:26,890
He speaks of a hero who
succumbed to his wounds.

128
00:07:26,890 --> 00:07:30,190
A death mask is taken of Rommel's face.

129
00:07:30,190 --> 00:07:35,100
For decades it lay in a display case
of the Rommel Museum in Herrlingen,

130
00:07:35,100 --> 00:07:41,020
until the municipality closed the museum in 2019
because the exhibition was considered outdated.

131
00:07:41,020 --> 00:07:45,580
The truth only comes out after
the war, through Manfred Rommel,

132
00:07:45,580 --> 00:07:48,470
who later becomes mayor of Stuttgart.

133
00:07:48,470 --> 00:07:52,430
After 1945, Rommel's fame keeps growing,

134
00:07:52,430 --> 00:07:55,270
above all through his former enemies.

135
00:07:55,270 --> 00:07:59,690
Books about him appear in Britain,
and Hollywood makes a film.

136
00:07:59,690 --> 00:08:03,910
The image of a fair war in
the desert suits the times:

137
00:08:03,910 --> 00:08:08,620
here, it seemed, there was no ideology,
only two armies in the sand.

138
00:08:08,620 --> 00:08:13,400
The new Bundeswehr also needs role models,
and barracks are named after him.

139
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:16,500
Historians today take a
much more critical view.

140
00:08:16,500 --> 00:08:21,700
He owed his steep rise to the leadership of
the time and was close to it for a long time.

141
00:08:21,700 --> 00:08:26,170
In North Africa the war was seen
as less brutal than in the East,

142
00:08:26,170 --> 00:08:28,190
but civilians suffered there too,

143
00:08:28,190 --> 00:08:32,860
and in Tunisia Jews under German
occupation were forced into labour.

144
00:08:32,860 --> 00:08:36,640
Other offices were responsible
for this, not Rommel's staff.

145
00:08:36,640 --> 00:08:38,440
How much he knew about it remains open.

146
00:08:38,440 --> 00:08:43,040
And some of the mines his
troops laid in North Africa

147
00:08:43,040 --> 00:08:45,670
are still in the ground today.

148
00:08:45,670 --> 00:08:51,320
In his hometown of Heidenheim, the figure
of a mine victim has stood next to his

149
00:08:51,320 --> 00:08:52,970
memorial since 2020.

150
00:08:52,970 --> 00:08:57,900
An officer who led from the
front and had a feel for images.

151
00:08:57,900 --> 00:09:01,870
A general who disobeyed orders
when he thought they were wrong,

152
00:09:01,870 --> 00:09:06,310
and who in the end was forced to
his death by his own leadership.

153
00:09:06,310 --> 00:09:08,110
How do you see Erwin Rommel?

154
00:09:08,110 --> 00:09:10,160
Tell us in the comments.

